    "result_text": "Done. The build is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec: on successful owner create, enqueue a welcome notification \u2014 emit via the logger named `NOTIFY` a line containing the owner id and the `memberId`.\n\n**1. New step class** \u2014 `EnqueueWelcomeNotification.java` (in the `owner` function package). A single-responsibility step following the existing `AuditOwnerCreated` pattern: it declares the owner via `@Val Owner` and logs one line to the dedicated `NOTIFY` logger with `owner.getId()` and `OwnerIdentity.primary(owner)` (the same single-source-of-truth accessor the audit trail uses for `memberId`, rather than re-reading the field directly).\n\n**2. Pipeline wiring** \u2014 inserted an `enqueueWelcome` step into `owners.POST.yml` after `record` and before `audit`. This placement guarantees the owner already carries its persisted id (it runs after `save`), and being inside the same transactional pipeline it only fires on a successful create \u2014 a failed/escalated request never reaches it.\n\nI reused existing infrastructure rather than duplicating: `OwnerIdentity.primary(...)` for the id resolution and the same SLF4J logger idiom as the audit steps. No existing class grew \u2014 the logic lives in its own small step.\n\nVerification: the `AcceptanceTest.coreEnqueuesWelcome` test (which captures the `NOTIFY` logger and asserts a line contains both the owner id and the fetched `memberId`) passes, and the full suite is green \u2014 the log confirms `NOTIFY - Welcome owner id=11 memberId=NSW2616E6609E3`.",
